# Part 1 – Baud Rate Generator

## Goal

Generate a timing signal that determines when the UART should process the next bit.

## Specifications

| Parameter |      Value      |
|-----------|-----------------|
| FPGA Clock |     100 MHz    |
| Baud Rate |     115200      |
| Clock Cycles per Bit | ~868 |

The relationship between the FPGA clock and baud rate is approximately:


100,000,000 / 115,200 ≈ 868


Therefore, approximately 868 FPGA clock cycles occur during one UART bit period.

## Architecture


100 MHz Clock
      │
      ▼
Clock Counter
      │
      ▼
 Baud Tick


The baud generator produces a timing pulse that is used by the transmitter and receiver to determine when to process the next UART bit.

## Concepts Learned

- Clock frequency
- Baud rate
- Clock divider
- Counter
- Clock enable
- Timing

## Simulation

## Reflection

The baud rate generator introduced the relationship between the FPGA clock and the timing required by a communication protocol.

I learned that the UART does not need a separate physical clock signal. Instead, the internal FPGA clock can be divided into smaller timing intervals that correspond to UART bit periods.

## Problems

The main challenge was understanding the relationship between the FPGA clock frequency and the desired baud rate.

Calculating the number of FPGA clock cycles per UART bit made the timing easier to understand.

