# Part 2 – UART Transmitter (TX)

## Goal

Build a UART transmitter that converts an 8-bit parallel value into a serial UART frame.

## Inputs

- data[7:0]
- start

## Output

- tx

## UART Transmission

The transmitter sends the bits in the following order:

Start → D0 → D1 → D2 → D3 → D4 → D5 → D6 → D7 → Stop

The least significant bit (LSB) is transmitted first.

## State Machine

IDLE
  │
  ▼
START
  │
  ▼
DATA
  │
  ▼
STOP
  │
  ▼
IDLE

## Concepts Learned

- UART transmission
- Serial data
- Start bit
- Stop bit
- LSB first
- FSM
- Baud timing

## Simulation

## Reflection

Building the transmitter showed how a parallel byte can be converted into a serial stream.

The FSM controls which part of the UART frame is currently being transmitted, while the baud timing determines when the next bit is sent.

This connected the FSM knowledge from the previous Sequential Logic section to a practical communication protocol.

## Problems

The main challenge was making sure that each bit remained on the TX line for the correct amount of time.

The baud tick was therefore important for synchronizing the state machine and bit transmission.

