# Part 3 – UART Receiver (RX)

## Goal

Build a UART receiver that converts an incoming serial UART frame into an 8-bit parallel value.

## Input

- rx


## Outputs

- data[7:0]
- data_valid


## UART Reception

The receiver waits for the start bit and then samples the incoming data bits.

Start
  ↓
D0 → D1 → D2 → D3 → D4 → D5 → D6 → D7
  ↓
Stop

## State Machine

IDLE
  │
  ▼
START
  │
  ▼
DATA
  │
  ▼
STOP
  │
  ▼
IDLE

## Concepts Learned

- UART reception
- Start bit detection
- Bit timing
- Serial-to-parallel conversion
- FSM
- Data validation

## Simulation

## Reflection

The receiver was more difficult to understand than the transmitter because it has to determine when the incoming serial data should be sampled.

I learned how the receiver uses the known baud timing to determine when each data bit should be read.

This also demonstrated why timing is one of the most important aspects of digital communication.

## Problems

The main challenge was correctly detecting the start bit and keeping the receiver synchronized with the incoming data.

Testing the receiver using simulation made it possible to inspect the timing of each bit and verify that the received byte was correct.

