# What I Learned

This project combined several concepts from the previous learning stages.

I learned how:

- A clock can be divided to create communication timing.
- Data can be transmitted one bit at a time.
- An FSM can control a communication protocol.
- A parallel byte can be converted into serial data.
- Serial data can be converted back into a parallel byte.
- Timing is critical when communicating between digital systems.

The UART project also showed how the simpler components built earlier can be combined into a larger hardware system.

---

# Challenges

The most challenging parts of the project were:

1. Understanding baud-rate timing.
2. Keeping TX synchronized with the baud generator.
3. Detecting the start bit in the receiver.
4. Sampling the received bits at the correct time.
5. Making sure the TX and RX modules worked correctly together.

Simulation and waveform inspection were used to identify and solve these problems.

---

# Reflection

UART was a significant step up from the smaller digital logic projects I had previously built.

The earlier projects focused mainly on individual components such as flip-flops, registers, counters and FSMs. In this project, those concepts had to work together as a complete communication system.

The project helped me understand how a real hardware interface can be constructed from relatively simple digital building blocks.

It also showed me the importance of simulation. Instead of only checking whether the Verilog compiled, I could inspect the actual timing and behavior of the signals and verify that the system worked correctly.

This project gives me a useful foundation for the next computer components, especially SPI, memory and the ALU.

